# regexp — real-world examples

Regular expressions resemble shell wildcards but on a much grander scale.
Supported by grep, sed, awk, find, and most programming languages.

## grep options for regex work

| Flag | Meaning |
|------|---------|
| `-i` | Ignore case (`--ignore-case`) |
| `-v` | Invert match: print NON-matching lines (`--invert-match`) |
| `-c` | Count matches instead of printing lines (`--count`) |
| `-l` | Print names of files that contain a match (`--files-with-matches`) |
| `-L` | Print names of files WITHOUT a match (`--files-without-match`) |
| `-n` | Prefix matches with line number (`--line-number`) |
| `-h` | Suppress filenames in multi-file output (`--no-filename`) |
| `-E` | Extended regex (no backslash-escaping `{}` `()` `|`) |

## Metacharacters

```
^ $ . [ ] { } - ? * + ( ) | \
```

## Anchors and character classes

```sh
grep -h '.zip' dirlist*.txt     # . = any one character: bunzip, bzip2 ...
grep -h '^zip' dirlist*.txt     # match at beginning of line
grep -h 'zip$' dirlist*.txt     # match at end of line
grep -h '[bg]zip' dirlist*.txt  # set: 'b' or 'g' before zip
grep -h '[^bg]zip' dirlist*.txt # negated set: any char EXCEPT b or g
grep -h '^[A-Z]' dirlist*.txt   # lines starting with a capital letter
```

## Quantifiers

```sh
grep '^lin'          # beginning of line
grep 'my$'           # end of line
grep '^[%*]'         # begins with % or *
grep '^[a-zA-Z]'     # starts with a letter
grep '^[^a-zA-Z]'    # does not start with a letter
grep '^.i'           # second letter is i
grep '^%*'           # zero or more % at line start (matches everything)
grep '^%\{1,4\}'     # begins with 1 to 4 %
grep '^1\{1\}'       # begins with exactly one 1
```

## Validate structured data (phone numbers)

```sh
# Generate a noisy test file
for i in {1..10}; do echo "(${RANDOM:0:3}) ${RANDOM:0:3}-${RANDOM:0:4}" >> phonelist.txt; done

# Print the MALFORMED entries (-v inverts, -E extended regex)
grep -Ev '^\([0-9]{3}\) [0-9]{3}-[0-9]{4}$' phonelist.txt
# (292) 108-518
# (129) 44-1379
```

## Regex with find and locate

```sh
# Files with characters outside the portable set
find . -regex '.*[^-_./0-9a-zA-Z].*'

# locate with regex alternation
locate --regex 'bin/(bz|gz|zip)'
```

## Shell tests, brace expansion, extglob (bonus)

```sh
# Directory-exists test
[ ! -d /home/david/media ] && printf '\nno such directory\n' || printf '\ndirectory exists\n'
[ ! -d /home/david/media ] && mkdir /home/david/media

# Brace expansion (not regex, but related shell matching)
echo Front-{A,B,C}-Back     # Front-A-Back Front-B-Back Front-C-Back
touch file{1..5}            # file1 ... file5

# extglob negation (shopt -s extglob)
rm !(2.txt)     # remove all files except 2.txt
rm !(*.pdf)     # remove all files except pdfs
```

## Homelab examples

```sh
# Strip comments and blank lines from any config
grep -v -e '^ *#' -e '^$' /etc/httpd/conf/httpd.conf

# Failed ssh logins on homelab, count by IP
journalctl -u sshd | grep -oE 'from [0-9.]+' | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head

# Find media files with episode numbering S01E02 style on mediaserver
ls /srv/media/tv | grep -E 'S[0-9]{2}E[0-9]{2}'
```
